fix(guard): the signing instructions are printed once whatever refusal comes first, and an Antigravity trim is bounded

Two findings of the cloud review of candidate 31 in the same denial-classes store.

The four grammar codes share one class so a context window pays for the listing once. The same flag also chose the human-override text, so after a read-spelling refusal (which prints the listing and offers no override) the first redirect or operator refusal got the short repeat text and the full signing instructions never appeared in that scope. One read-and-record call (denialFirstOccurrences) now answers both questions: the listing follows the shared class, the override text follows whether it was actually printed once in this scope (overrideShown, set only by a refusal that offered the override). A refusal with a typed retry action instead does not count as having shown it. isFirstDenialThisScope stays as the listing answer.

No Antigravity hook reports a compaction or a resume (all five were read), so a conversation-keyed scope could keep trimming a listing that had left the context. A heuristic bounds it: 30 minutes after the last FULL listing (listingAtMs, not the file's mtime, which moves when any class is recorded) the scope forgets what it has seen and the next refusal is full again. A store without the clock, or with a clock in the future, is full once. It only adds output and applies to the conversation key only; the session-keyed scope and its compaction reset are unchanged and its store keeps its shape. Cases pin the two-answer call, the retry-action case, the rendered refusals, the window, the not-the-mtime rule and the fail-open edges.

